<%# Body tab of the Bodies & Food page %>
<p class="page-subtitle">From trunk to tusks to toes, elephants have amazing bodies. Let's take a closer look!</p>

<div class="section">
  <span class="section-label">Wow facts</span>
  <div class="stat-row">
    <div class="stat-card" data-mascot-fact="The heaviest elephant ever recorded weighed about 10,900 kg, roughly as much as 150 adult people!">
      <div class="stat-label">Weight</div>
      <div class="stat-value">6,000 kg</div>
      <p>A big male African elephant weighs about as much as 4 cars!</p>
      <%= copy_button "Weight" %>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card" data-mascot-fact="The oldest known elephant, Lin Wang, lived to 86 at a zoo in Taiwan.">
      <div class="stat-label">Lifespan</div>
      <div class="stat-value">60–70 years</div>
      <p>Wild elephants can live to be as old as your grandparents.</p>
      <%= copy_button "Lifespan (wow fact)" %>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card" data-mascot-fact="An elephant can breathe in through its trunk at over 500 km/h, about 30 times faster than a human sneeze.">
      <div class="stat-label">Trunk</div>
      <div class="stat-value">~40,000</div>
      <p>Muscle bundles in an elephant's trunk. Your whole body has only about 600 muscles!</p>
      <%= copy_button "Trunk" %>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>How big are elephants?</h2>
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Kind of elephant</th>
        <th>How tall (grown-up male, to the shoulder)</th>
        <th>How heavy (grown-up male)</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>African savanna elephant</strong></td>
        <td>3–4 m (10–13 ft)</td>
        <td>4,500–6,800 kg</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>African forest elephant</strong></td>
        <td>2.2–2.5 m (7–8 ft)</td>
        <td>2,000–4,000 kg</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Asian elephant</strong></td>
        <td>2.5–3 m (8–10 ft)</td>
        <td>3,500–5,000 kg</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Elephants are the largest land animals on Earth. The biggest savanna elephants are taller than a basketball hoop! Females are smaller than males. A baby elephant,
    called a <em>calf</em>, weighs about 100 kg when it's born. That's more than a grown-up person!
  </p>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>Growing up and breathing</h2>
  <ul class="fact-list">
    <li data-mascot-fact="You can estimate an elephant&#x27;s age by checking which of its six sets of molars it&#x27;s using.">
      <span class="fact-label">Lifespan</span>
      <span class="fact-value">
        Wild elephants usually live <strong>60–70 years</strong>. An elephant grows six sets of big back teeth in its life.
        When the last set wears out, it can't chew its food anymore.
      </span>
      <%= copy_button "Lifespan" %>
    </li>
    <li data-mascot-fact="A female elephant usually has one calf every 4 to 5 years.">
      <span class="fact-label">Pregnancy</span>
      <span class="fact-value">About <strong>22 months</strong>, the longest of any mammal.</span>
      <%= copy_button "Pregnancy" %>
    </li>
    <li data-mascot-fact="An elephant&#x27;s trunk has two nostrils that run all the way along it.">
      <span class="fact-label">Breathing</span>
      <span class="fact-value">
        Elephants mostly breathe through their trunk, which is their nose. They can swim, too! When they swim, they hold
        the tip of their trunk above the water like a snorkel. Their lungs are built differently from ours, which helps
        them do this. They can also breathe through their mouth.
      </span>
      <%= copy_button "Breathing" %>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <%= render "shared/gallery_photo", slug: "mother-and-calf" %>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>Amazing body parts</h2>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card" data-mascot-fact="Baby elephants don&#x27;t know how to use their trunks at first, and sometimes suck on them like a child sucking its thumb.">
      <h3>The trunk</h3>
      <p>
        A trunk is a nose and top lip joined together. It has no bones, but about 40,000 small muscle groups. Elephants use
        it to breathe, smell, drink, grab food, say hello and spray water or dust. African elephants have two "fingers" at
        the tip. Asian elephants have one.
      </p>
      <%= copy_button "The trunk" %>
    </div>
    <div class="card" data-mascot-fact="Because of poaching, more elephants are being born without tusks in some places, such as Gorongosa National Park in Mozambique.">
      <h3>Tusks</h3>
      <p>
        Tusks are giant front teeth that never stop growing. Elephants use them to dig, peel bark, lift things and protect
        themselves. Just like you might be left- or right-handed, elephants can be left- or right-tusked!
      </p>
      <%= copy_button "Tusks" %>
    </div>
    <div class="card" data-mascot-fact="Every elephant&#x27;s ears have a unique pattern of veins, nicks and tears that researchers use to tell individuals apart.">
      <h3>Ears</h3>
      <p>Big ears help elephants keep cool. When they flap their ears, the blood inside cools down, and that cools the whole elephant.</p>
      <%= copy_button "Ears" %>
    </div>
    <div class="card" data-mascot-fact="An elephant&#x27;s foot spreads wider when it takes weight and narrows when lifted, so it doesn&#x27;t get stuck in mud.">
      <h3>Feet</h3>
      <p>Elephants walk on tiptoe! A thick, squishy pad under each foot works like a cushion. Their feet can even feel rumbles travelling through the ground.</p>
      <%= copy_button "Feet" %>
    </div>
    <div class="card" data-mascot-fact="The tiny cracks in African elephant skin hold 5 to 10 times more water than smooth skin would.">
      <h3>Skin</h3>
      <p>In some places it's 2.5 cm thick, but it can still feel a fly land and get sunburnt. Wrinkles hold water and mud to keep it cool.</p>
      <%= copy_button "Skin" %>
    </div>
    <div class="card" data-mascot-fact="Elephants can recognise themselves in a mirror, something only a few animals can do.">
      <h3>Brain</h3>
      <p>An elephant's brain weighs about 5 kg, the biggest of any land animal. That's almost 4 times heavier than a person's! Elephants are famous for their great memory.</p>
      <%= copy_button "Brain" %>
    </div>
  </div>
  <%= render "shared/gallery_photo", slug: "mud-bath" %>
  <figure class="photo-block">
    <%= image_tag "pages/anatomy-skeleton.png", alt: "Skeleton of an Asian elephant shown next to a human silhouette for scale", class: "photo-block-image" %>
    <figcaption>
      <span>An elephant's skeleton, shown next to a person for scale.</span>
      <%= copy_picture_button "pages/anatomy-skeleton.png", "elephant skeleton" %>
    </figcaption>
    <%= render "shared/image_credit", path: "pages/anatomy-skeleton.png" %>
  </figure>
</div>

<%= render "shared/learn_more", page: :anatomy %>
